{
  "beruf": "kita",
  "title": "Cleaning and domestic work",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "spanish-for-work run, 2026-11-07: written by hand for Spain, read once by an AI second reader, not yet by a human. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon or in another deck are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Cleaning offices, hotels and homes is a common first job in Spain, and so is working for a family, sometimes looking after their children. This deck has products and tools, the jobs in a house or an office, the working day, safety with chemicals, and how to talk with the family or your employer. For domestic work, usted or tú depends on the family: ask.",
  "introEasy": "Do you clean houses or offices, or work for a family in Spain? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Limpiador/a",
    "Empleada/o de hogar",
    "Cuidador/a de niños",
    "Limpiador/a de oficinas",
    "Canguro"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "On your first day, ask how the family or client likes things done: «¿Qué productos uso para el suelo?», «¿Por dónde empiezo?». It avoids most problems.",
    "Never mix lejía with other products, especially amoniaco: the fumes are dangerous. Open a window and read the label.",
    "Domestic workers in Spain have their own set of rules and a special social security scheme (the sistema especial para empleados de hogar). They change, so ask about your contract, hours and social security at the Seguridad Social, a union or a gestoría. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
